<section class="event-challenge" aria-labelledby="challenge-title">
  <header>
    <p>The challenge</p>
    <div>
      <h2 id="challenge-title">Why ripple and IED separation is difficult</h2>
      <p>
        Hippocampal ripples and interictal epileptiform discharges are brief, spontaneous events that can overlap in waveform shape, amplitude, and frequency content. Conventional detectors usually identify 
        candidate events using thresholds on ripple- or IED-band activity, but these measures capture only part of the distinction between the two. Large ripple complexes, sharp transients, and atypical 
        events can produce similar threshold responses despite having different underlying structure. Separating them reliably therefore depends on the full temporal and multichannel morphology of each event, 
        and on whether those features generalize across subjects. The problem is not only to detect high-frequency events, but to distinguish between two partially overlapping physiological phenomena and reject 
        events that do not clearly match either class.
      </p>
    </div>
  </header>
</section>

